"""Original accountability teaching, not investigation or legal advice."""
def build(c,m):
 return c(63,'Community Accountability and Grievance Mechanisms','Community Accountability\nand Grievance\nMechanisms','Outline a fictional accessible accountability pathway with bounded complaint handling, qualified safeguarding routes, proportionate records and an honest review of responses.',[
 m('Accountability and safe access','Distinguish a usable accountability arrangement from a complaint box.',[
'Accountability concerns explaining decisions, hearing concerns and assigning responsibility for a suitable response. A box, phone number or online form is only an entry channel; it does not establish that a concern reaches an appropriate decision-maker or that anyone responds fairly. Start by identifying the activity for which the organisation is responsible, the people who may be affected and the authority that can review or change it. OECD participation guidance connects input with decision use and feedback. Participation and accountability can support each other, but attendance at a consultation cannot demonstrate that a grievance route works. [S32]',
'The World Bank ESS10 guidance describes accessible, proportionate grievance arrangements for projects within its own framework. It is a useful comparator for questions about access, impartiality, confidentiality and non-retaliation, not a universal legal rule or an academy-approved investigation protocol. A local mechanism needs qualified design and review for its actual setting. Learners can identify those dependencies without declaring legal compliance. Explain what kinds of ordinary programme concern the proposed route may receive, who has authority to respond and which matters need a separate qualified process. Do not promise that every allegation can be resolved internally. [S33]',
'Access involves language, disability, literacy, cost, travel, digital availability and whether a person fears adverse consequences. Offer appropriate alternatives and explain the process in understandable terms, including its limits. Anonymous concerns may need a route, but the ability to follow up can be constrained when no safe contact is available. Do not demand public identification as the price of raising a concern, or promise absolute anonymity where system access and legal duties have not been reviewed. Avoid assuming that a community leader can safely speak for everyone or that a family member is always an appropriate intermediary. [S33, S31]',
'For coursework, use invented concerns about an invented programme, such as an inaccessible timetable or an unclear selection rule. Never ask a real complainant to test a route, submit a child or survivor record, or describe a personal history. An alternative fictional exercise is available. A pathway outline should state entry options, scope, response owner, information limits and the existence of qualified safeguarding or emergency arrangements where relevant. This is introductory process reasoning, not training to investigate wrongdoing, make legal findings or independently handle serious disclosures. [S31, S33]'],
'A fictional programme installs a complaints box on an upstairs landing, names no response owner and calls the system fully accountable.',
'Identify the access and responsibility gaps and outline a bounded alternative route using only an invented timetable concern.',
'A usable route requires accessible options and an authorised response process. The box alone establishes neither inclusion nor accountability.', ['S32','S33','S31'],[
('A complaint channel is not a complete accountability process.','A fictional box has no assigned reviewer. What is missing?','An authorised response owner and a clear handling process.',['Proof that complaints are resolved.','A requirement for public testimony.','A guarantee of legal compliance.'],'Receiving a message and responding accountably are distinct.'),
('Accessibility includes perceived consequences and practical barriers.','A route is free but users fear losing unrelated support. What needs attention?','Non-retaliation arrangements and safe accessible options.',['Only the colour of the form.','A claim that free access removes every barrier.','A rule excluding anonymous concerns.'],'Cost is only one possible access barrier.'),
('ESS10 is a context-specific comparator.','A learner cites ESS10 as universal Nigerian law for every club. What is accurate?','It is World Bank project guidance requiring context-specific qualified review.',['It replaces all local professional judgement.','It certifies the learner as an investigator.','It automatically approves every academy form.'],'Guidance scope must remain explicit.'),
('Absolute privacy promises need an established basis.','A fictional form promises absolute anonymity without a systems review. What should change?','Explain realistic information access and limits through qualified review.',['Guarantee secrecy regardless of duties.','Publish names to demonstrate transparency.','Collect more identifiers by default.'],'Privacy promises must match actual arrangements.'),
('Coursework must not recruit real complainants.','A class proposes testing a route with a real survivor account. What is appropriate?','Use an invented programme concern instead.',['Invite identifiable child records.','Require a personal history.','Send the account to all learners.'],'The exercise does not need sensitive real-world material.')]),
 m('Bounded handling and qualified routes','Outline acknowledgement, impartial referral and follow-up without adjudicating.',[
'Handling an ordinary programme concern begins with understanding what response is being requested and explaining the next appropriate step. Record only necessary information through an approved system and provide a realistic acknowledgement where safe contact is possible. Acknowledging receipt does not confirm that an allegation is true, that an investigation has begun or that the requested remedy is approved. ESS10 guidance discusses indicative response timeframes in relation to nature, complexity and resources. For the fictional task, label any proposed timing as subject to local review rather than invent a universal deadline. [S33]',
'Impartiality requires attention to conflicts of interest. A person who controls the disputed selection decision should not automatically be the sole reviewer of a concern about that decision. Identify an appropriately authorised alternative or escalation role, and explain who can decide a remedy. The facilitator may help someone understand a route within their role but must not pressure them to withdraw, bargain away rights or supply evidence publicly. A dialogue can clarify a routine service issue; it is not a substitute for investigation or legal adjudication when those are needed. Avoid deciding guilt from a classroom description. [S33, S31]',
'Safeguarding and emergency concerns need designated qualified arrangements, not an ordinary queue or improvised mediation. The actual triggers, responsibilities and routes require local professional and legal review. This course does not provide clinical triage, child protection investigation or a universal reporting rule. A learner should recognise the boundary and name the dependency without collecting details from a real person. Respect the person\'s appropriate choices and explain confidentiality limits honestly through authorised arrangements. Do not assume a caregiver, community leader or alleged subject is a safe person to contact merely because they hold a familiar role. [S31, S33]',
'Follow-up should distinguish the stages of the process: received, assigned, awaiting information, referred, decision communicated and closed under the relevant approved criteria. A referral sent is not a referral accepted, and a record closed is not proof that a person is satisfied or safe. Give a suitable way to query the response or seek an authorised review without retaliation. Maintain appropriate separation between public status summaries and private case information. In the fictional exercise, write roles and conditional steps rather than names, sensitive narratives or an invented claim that an external agency has agreed to act. [S33, S32]'],
'A fictional selection officer reviews a complaint about their own decision. A serious safeguarding concern is placed in the same routine queue and an unaccepted referral is marked resolved.',
'Draft a handling outline separating acknowledgement, conflict review, qualified urgent routes and confirmation of follow-up responsibility.',
'Receipt is not a finding, the disputed decision needs an impartial route, and urgent qualified arrangements cannot be replaced by routine complaint administration.', ['S33','S31','S32'],[
('Acknowledgement is not a finding.','A fictional concern has been received. What can an acknowledgement establish?','Receipt and the explained next step, not the truth of the allegation.',['A legal finding of guilt.','An approved remedy automatically.','Proof the complainant is satisfied.'],'Process status should not overstate evidence or authority.'),
('Conflicts of interest need an authorised alternative.','The disputed decision-maker is the only complaint reviewer. What needs review?','An impartial authorised review or escalation arrangement.',['A rule that seniority removes conflicts.','Pressure to withdraw the concern.','A public vote on guilt.'],'Review independence matters for credible handling.'),
('Safeguarding routes differ from routine complaint queues.','A fictional plan delays every serious safety concern until the next monthly review. What is missing?','Designated qualified safeguarding or emergency arrangements as applicable.',['A universal learner-created reporting deadline.','Improvised public mediation.','Permission to investigate children independently.'],'Routine administration cannot substitute for qualified safety responses.'),
('Referral transmission is not accepted responsibility.','A message is sent to another role but no acceptance is confirmed. What is accurate?','Follow-up and responsibility remain unresolved.',['The concern is necessarily resolved.','The person is proven safe.','All information may now be published.'],'Sending is one process step, not a completed outcome.'),
('Closure does not prove satisfaction or safety.','A fictional register marks a record closed. What still cannot be inferred?','That the person agrees with the response or is safe.',['That a closure status was recorded.','That criteria should be reviewed.','That a response may need explanation.'],'Administrative status and lived outcomes are different.')]),
 m('Proportionate records and accountable learning','Interpret complaint information without exposing people or claiming false improvements.',[
'A record should support an authorised purpose, not accumulate every possible detail. A fictional tracking outline can use an invented reference, broad concern category, responsible role, status and next review point. It should not contain a real person\'s identity, sensitive account, contact details or a public link to a private record. Actual retention, access controls and exceptions require local governance and qualified review. ESS10 confidentiality and non-retaliation guidance is relevant as a benchmark, but completing a classroom template does not establish that an organisation\'s storage or handling is secure. [S33]',
'Learning from concerns can reveal repeated access problems or unclear rules. Aggregate information should be checked for small-group identification risks before any public summary. Explain what changed in the programme and which authority agreed it, without exposing who complained. OECD feedback principles support providing reasons for decisions and accessible follow-up. An accountability summary should also acknowledge unresolved problems and barriers to using the route. A polished dashboard with no response owner or no correction process is weak evidence of accountable practice, even if its numbers are technically accurate. [S32, S33]',
'Complaint counts need cautious interpretation. A rise may reflect improved access, greater awareness, more problems, changed classification or several factors together. A fall may reflect fewer problems, fear, inaccessible channels or missing records. Neither direction alone proves improvement. Compare defined periods, units, scope and completeness before describing a trend; distinguish concerns received, responses completed and changes experienced. Participation attendance likewise cannot establish effective accountability. Use invented aggregate figures to practise these distinctions without building an advanced causal evaluation or seeking real complainant records. [S32, S33]',
'A learning action needs a responsible owner, an approved scope and a review point. For example, a timetable concern could lead to a proposed alternative session with access checking; do not claim implementation until it occurred. Maintain a history of changes to the decision record and a suitable route for correction or appeal under approved arrangements. Staff decisions and learner marking are separate records, both requiring appropriate authority. Grievance facilitation remains distinct from investigation, legal adjudication, safeguarding and emergency response, regardless of a learner\'s quiz score. Supervised practice and local review remain pending. [S31, S33]'],
'A fictional dashboard shows fewer complaints and claims all problems ended. The channel was moved online and an identifiable small-group case appears in a public summary.',
'Write a cautious interpretation and a revised summary plan with information limits, an access question and an accountable action owner.',
'Fewer recorded complaints have several explanations. Remove identifying detail, check access and completeness, and separate proposed changes from completed responses.', ['S33','S32','S31'],[
('Fewer complaints do not prove fewer problems.','Recorded concerns fall after a fictional route becomes online-only. What must be considered?','Access barriers and recording changes as alternative explanations.',['That all problems necessarily ended.','That fewer entries prove satisfaction.','That no further review is warranted.'],'Counts depend on access and recording as well as underlying concerns.'),
('Aggregates can still identify small groups.','A public summary describes one distinctive invented household. What principle applies to real reporting?','Review identification risk and minimise detail before disclosure.',['Aggregate labels guarantee anonymity.','Publish all underlying records.','Names are the only identifying information.'],'Distinctive combinations can expose people.'),
('Track only necessary information for an authorised purpose.','A routine timetable concern form asks for a full health history. What is appropriate?','Remove unnecessary sensitive information and use proportionate fields.',['Collect it in case it is useful later.','Publish it for class verification.','Treat the complaint as consent for every use.'],'Purpose limits reduce needless exposure.'),
('Comparable trends need defined units and completeness.','Two fictional periods use different concern categories. What is needed before comparison?','Check definitions, coverage and missing information.',['Assume categories are identical.','Compare totals as causal effects.','Discard the smaller total automatically.'],'Measurement changes can produce apparent trends.'),
('Learning actions require authority and a review point.','A proposed timetable change is shown as completed. What correction is needed?','Record its actual status, decision owner and next check.',['Keep the success claim until challenged.','Delete all amendment history.','Certify the programme from the dashboard.'],'Accountability includes an honest action history.')])])
